  "narration": {
    "title": "Elly De La Cruz Torches a Week to Remember",
    "intro": "The week belonged to Elly De La Cruz, who went 12-for-24 with five homers for the Reds. Behind him, a few big bats cooled off and a handful of arms slipped a tier. Here's who's trending which way.",
    "hweek_lead": "De La Cruz was untouchable, a 1.746 OPS across six games with five homers and 10 RBI. Rafael Devers wasn't far off in San Francisco at a 1.605 mark on three homers, and Travis Bazzana and Teoscar Hern\u00e1ndez both cleared 1.45 for the week too.",
    "risers_lead": "The home run board moved fastest at the top: Elly De La Cruz added five to reach 26, and Kyle Schwarber tacked on four to sit at 44. Rafael Devers climbed three to 35, so a fast week didn't just show up in the OPS columns.",
    "fallers_lead": "A few good bats simply went quiet. Bryce Harper managed 4-for-25 over seven games and slipped .024 in season OPS, with Michael Harris II (2-for-23) and Matt Olson in the same boat. None of it is alarming, just a cold week for names used to better.",
    "pfallers_lead": "On the mound, Foster Griffin and Sean Burke each shed seven form points to land at 60, and Keider Montero dropped five to 68. Arrows pointing down, but all three are still in workable territory.",
    "colors_lead": "Only one tier change of note: Robbie Ray slid from above-average to average. A small step, not a collapse."
  }
}